WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.610
Everyone knows that one fan's trash is another
fan's treasure, and even the worst moments

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.500
in film history can serve a purpose.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:10.179
So maybe that's why these deleted scenes are
so fascinating — because they bear such

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:15.040
a hilarious and terrifying reminder of how
easily one scene could destroy a whole movie.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.270
The Star Wars prequels are pretty much the
definition of a mixed bag.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.740
On the one hand, the visuals are often stunning,
they have no shortage of bold ideas, and the

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.080
lightsaber duels are nothing short of spectacular.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.260
On the other hand…

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.120
But this truly baffling deleted scene from
Revenge of the Sith is a terrifying reminder

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.640
that, as bad as the prequels could be at times,
they could have been so much worse.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.960
The scene takes place after Anakin and Obi
Wan board General Grievous' ship to rescue

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.660
the kidnapped Chancellor Palpatine.

00:00:47.989 --> 00:00:52.300
The two Jedi are unsure of which way to go,
so they contact everyone's favorite droid,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.680
R2D2, for directions.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.300
But they're stumped — because their communicator
can't translate his beeps and bops into actual

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:58.800
words.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:00:59.800
And then this happens:

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.480
"I'm pretty sure that beep is down.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.380
“I sense Count Dooku is above us.”

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.600
“Oh yes, yes you’re right beep is up."

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.920
It goes without saying that this is the only
time we've ever seen a human do that in Star

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:12.420
Wars.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.320
The moment is played for laughs and it is
kind of weirdly charming, but it also makes

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.820
absolutely zero sense and completely breaks
the reality of the film.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.640
Even though it's probably a good thing that
this clip was unearthed and put on the internet

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.130
— so that everyone can marvel at how gloriously
dumb it is — it's probably also a good thing

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.510
that it didn't actually make it into Star
Wars canon.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.360
Back to the Future has gone down in history
as a classic movie for good reason.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.700
Not only does it succeed at being a thrilling
sci-fi adventure story and a hilarious comedy

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.960
at the same time, but it also has a genuinely
good heart — and not many movies can say

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:44.460
all that.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.640
But there are certain elements of the film
that haven't aged well, and undermine how

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.920
sweet the rest of it is.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.760
In particular, Back to the Future isn't great
in terms of its portrayal of gender and sexuality.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.440
It's one thing for Biff to be a predator — that's
obviously terrible, but he is at least clearly

00:01:59.440 --> 00:01:59.940
a villain.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.280
It's quite another thing, however, that the
theoretically adorable young George McFly's

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.500
horrifying hobby of spying on undressing women
is played for laughs.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.000
As if that wasn't enough, the film had an
additional cringe-worthy deleted scene that

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.170
might have ruined the characters of Doc Brown
and Marty McFly if the filmmakers had left

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:16.660
it in.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.800
In this scene, as Doc Brown attempts to get
Marty ready for his fake date with his mother,

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.840
Doc makes a less-than-stellar comment which
really does deserve to be left in the past.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.959
"I just don't know if I can go through with
it, hitting on her.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.260
Nobody said anything about hitting her, you’re
just gonna take a few liberties with her."

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.980
Then after that, Marty worries out loud how
putting moves on his mom might turn him gay.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.480
Just be glad we ended up in the timeline where
this horrible scene was erased from existence.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.500
In The Hobbit: An Unexpected Journey, Bilbo
and his dwarvish companions encounter a series

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.560
of difficult trials on their journey to slay
the dragon Smaug.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:56.420
One of the most difficult spots in which these
heroes find themselves comes when they're

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.180
captured by goblins in the mountains.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.340
As dire as things seem for them in the finished
movie, there was a deleted scene in which

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:06.069
the king of the goblins forces the dwarves
to endure a form of torture that was deemed

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:10.220
too cruel and unusual to depict on screen...
a goblin musical number.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:15.120
The song is called "Down in Goblin Town,"
and there's no way to sugarcoat this: it's

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:15.640
terrible.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.820
You could argue that it's supposed to be bad.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.200
“It’s one of my own compositions.”

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.120
"That's not a song.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.720
It's an abomination!"

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:30.090
It's based on an intentionally atonal song
from the original book, and does a good job

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:34.410
of characterizing the goblins as weird little
monsters and their king as a self-important

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.240
diva who doesn't care for the well-being of
his captive audience.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.520
But that doesn't make it okay to be bad.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.420
It's also two full minutes long, and nothing
actually happens during those two minutes

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.740
that's relevant to the plot.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.920
In a movie that's already almost three hours
long, this scene is almost insultingly superfluous.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:54.240
Of course, as seems inevitable with any Peter
Jackson Tolkien adaptation, the scene was

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.160
later re-inserted into the film as part of
an extended version.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.160
Given that most of the cast of the original
Ghostbusters were already established comedians

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.920
who were veterans of Saturday Night Live,
it's no surprise that a decent portion of

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.220
the dialogue was improvised over the course
of filming.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.680
But director Ivan Reitman clearly wasn't sure
just how absurd the movie's comedy should

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:13.180
be.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.280
Among the failed comedic experiments that
he was toying around with was one particular

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.940
subplot that was, in retrospect, gloriously
ill-conceived.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.540
In the book Making Ghostbusters, Reitman explains
that he had planned to have Bill Murray and

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.990
Dan Aykroyd play dual roles, not only as the
Ghostbusters Peter Venkman and Ray Stantz,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:35.220
but also a pair of homeless men named Bojay
and Coombs that were supposed to serve as

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.460
a kind of mundane counterpoint to the otherwise
world-ending action.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:47.780
"Run, get out of the way! Rush rush rush,
always rushing."

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.740
Rather than grounding the movie, however,
Reitman immediately knew upon watching Murray

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:56.150
and Aykroyd on film that focusing on two side
characters who were inexplicably played by

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:58.840
the lead actors would have the completely
opposite effect.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:03.100
However, these characters were present in
the novelization of the film, probably because

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.840
book characters can't suffer from weird casting.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:11.620
Ridley Scott's 1979 film Alien is beloved
as not only a work of thought-provoking science

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.260
fiction, but also a tremendously spooky horror
film.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.040
Along with Jaws, Alien is famous for solidifying
the conventional wisdom among horror movie

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.120
aficionados that the less of the monster you
see, the better.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.100
There was, however, a scene deleted from the
middle of Alien that violated this sacred

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.550
tenet of filmmaking, and proved just how quickly
scary can become silly if a movie shows too

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:31.580
much too soon.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:35.820
In this scene, while trying to escape the
ship, Lambert stumbles upon the alien sitting

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:36.420
on the ground.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:41.400
She stares at it for a moment, paralyzed with
fear, before the alien… kinda just crab-walks

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.040
over to her.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:46.300
It's probably supposed to be tense, but from
the way the scene is framed and paced, it

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:49.560
ends up being totally ridiculous — and not
even remotely scary.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:53.800
And as cool the effects were in Alien, this
scene is a reminder that terrifying creatures

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.400
look way better in quick glimpses, allowing
your mind can fill in the details.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.460
When you have the chance to see the alien's
entire body for a long period of time in a

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:06.240
well-lit room, it quickly becomes very clear
you're just looking at a guy in a rubber suit.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:12.160
The setting of Who Framed Roger Rabbit seems
absurd at first glance, because it is: a film

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:16.270
noir version of Los Angeles in which real
people live alongside cartoons as separate

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:17.320
social classes.

00:06:17.729 --> 00:06:22.259
Despite this strange premise, the worldbuilding
ends up being remarkably consistent, and it's

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.980
pretty easy to get your head around it once
the story gets going.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:29.090
But that might not have been the case if one
sanity-shattering scene had made it through

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.000
the final edit.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.980
After being captured by the villains, protagonist
Eddie Valiant finds that his head has been

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:37.780
trapped inside a cartoon pig head that seems
to be at least partially alive.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.560
"Oh, God!

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:43.060
OH!"

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:47.880
After freaking out for a moment, Eddie escapes
from his cartoon confinement in an equally

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.160
cartoon-like way.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:54.500
Relative to all the other weird gags in this
movie, this one is much harder to understand,

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.560
largely because it doesn't seem to be based
on anything from pre-existing cartoon convention.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.020
Was the pig head once a part of larger organism
that was beheaded?

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:05.700
Or did the bad guys somehow create a sentient
severed pig head from nothing?

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:08.320
Did Eddie kill the creature when he washed
it down the drain?

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:12.580
There's no way to answer these questions without
getting into some really strange existential

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.080
territory.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:17.660
Perhaps the wildest thing about this scene
is that, even with hindsight, director Robert

00:07:17.669 --> 00:07:20.360
Zemeckis doesn't seem to think it was all
that weird.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.180
the only reason he cut it was because it slowed
down the movie.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.500
Released during the same summer as Iron Man,
Hancock is something of a forgotten oddity,

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.560
from a time before the MCU had redefined superhero
movies.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.100
It stars Will Smith and Charlize Theron, and
has a script co-written by Vince Gilligan,

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.100
who later went on to create Breaking Bad.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:42.180
Although the film only got mixed reviews when
it came out, no one could fault it for lacking

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:42.780
ambition.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:47.380
It was a bold deconstruction of superhero
conventions, with no costumes, codenames,

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:51.720
or explosions, aiming to be more of a comedy/drama
than a nonstop action movie.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.480
Nevertheless, one deleted scene did feature
an explosion of sorts.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:59.760
During this scene, the depressed alcoholic
superhero Hancock is having a casual fling

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.420
with a woman he doesn't really know, fooling
around with her on the couch in his mobile

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:03.920
home.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:07.600
Before things go further, however, he tries
to warn her about some of the side-effects

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.680
that may occur during their impending encounter.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.060
"Start climbing the mountaintop you know.

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:19.520
You’re gonna need to be as far away from
that as possible."

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.400
Unfortunately, she's far too eager to heed
his warnings.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.440
So she's thrown across the room while Hancock
recovers.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:29.660
Considering the scene is nothing more than
a long set up to a silly joke, it probably

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:31.720
deserved to be left on the cutting room floor.

00:08:32.839 --> 00:08:36.390
Although the Terminator films are generally
pretty grim, they're not afraid of getting

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.099
a little goofy every once in while.

00:08:38.099 --> 00:08:41.960
But one scene deleted from Terminator 3: Rise
of the Machines might have taken things a

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:43.000
little too far.

00:08:43.529 --> 00:08:47.240
The scene depicts a promotional video for
"Cyber Research Systems," the company that

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:48.740
developed the original Terminators.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.860
In case you don't remember, Terminators are
killer robots from the future, many of whom

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.500
are designed to look and sound like Arnold
Schwarzenegger.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:59.940
And this video introduces its audience to
the human whose likeness was used as the base

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:05.260
for these robots: Chief Master Sergeant William
Candy, who's voiced by Samuel L. Jackson.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.020
"It is now within our power to make war safe."

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.880
The real icing on the cake, however, is when
one of the politicians watching the video

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:14.680
complains about Candy's accent.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:16.960
"I don't know about that accent.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:19.940
“We can fix it."

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.460
The whole affair is completely unnecessary,
thoroughly dumb, and utterly delightful.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.820
Even though it was cut, many Terminator fans
have accepted this wonderfully weird scene

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:33.480
as canon, and William Candy has his own entry
on the Terminator Wiki.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:39.480
James Cameron's 1997 blockbuster hit film
Titanic is no stranger to the concept of dramatic

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:39.980
irony.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:42.500
"So this is the ship they say is unsinkable.”

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.580
“God himself could not sink this ship."

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.920
This concept works pretty well for Titanic,
however, considering it's clear from the beginning

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.080
how things are going to end.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:55.930
In case you forgot your high school lit, dramatic
irony is a literary device in which the audience

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:59.839
has more knowledge of the events of a story
than the characters do, which lends these

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:03.520
character's words an additional layer of meaning
they themselves don't understand.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:08.290
When writing a historical fiction epic like
Titanic, the temptation to write nothing but

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:11.220
jokey lines that wink at the audience must
be overwhelming.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.820
Generally, however, Cameron does a good job
of limiting his use of dramatic irony — save

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:17.440
for one particular instance.

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:19.860
"Hey sonny, how about a little ice?"

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:29.480
Needless to say, Cameron eventually decided
that this, the most dramatic moment of the

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:32.640
film, was not the right time for a really
terrible Dad joke.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:34.660
Probably a solid decision.

